Subject: [PATCH] Fritchii v5: render target relocation, cold sweep of drifted chains, render-frame clock and tombstones, v2 with the review fixes (EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_RELOCATE / _COLD / _CLOCK / _TOMBSTONE, all off by default)

Replaces fritchii-relocate.diff (same switches; do not stack the two). v2 fixes the three major
defects of the independent review (2026-11-09); see "v2" below.

Port of Frizti/Fritchii (author Frizti, with Claude Opus 5.5), v5 "moving NPCs no longer drag fps"
and "~2.4 GB less video memory" (fable_answer_12 2A/2C, fable_answer_16 1b/1c/3.2, fable_answer_17):
  c98e63520a  relocation guard bits + candidate rule (EXP43 groundwork)
  07b38b3caf  RelocateImage (EXP43=2/3)          1735a6fd13  EXP43=3 on by default upstream
  ba95b54e3b  cold sweep (EDEN_NX2_TC_COLD_FRAMES) 8ccfd05519  clock and cold sweep (K=4) on by default upstream
  e722284eec, a3f5cb27d4, ddc5e2585c  render-frame clock (flip-fence source, nx2_frame_fence.h)
  86a04911af  EXP43=4 relocation tombstones
One diff because the cold sweep, the clock and the tombstones all live on the relocation guard
(RelocationShaped, relocation_shapes, last_write_frame, guard bits); each part has its own switch.

Switches (Common::PeSwitch, exact "1"; all unset = the same behaviour as T82: only a few cached
bool checks, and one relaxed atomic increment per nx2:syncpt session for its id):
- EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_RELOCATE=1: upstream EXP43=3, and always the render-frame clock (v2). A render
  target that a clear overwrites whole (overwritten_by_clear, moved above the image creation,
  unchanged), with no copies, aliases or remapped images among its overlaps, takes over the most
  recently modified bad overlap of its own single-level single-layer 2D shape and layout that
  drifted less than its size and was not written in this render frame: RelocateImage keeps its
  VkImage, memory, views and framebuffers (MarkStale, untrack, unregister, overlap links dropped,
  out of the old alloc entry, views out of the bound targets and descriptor lookups, new address
  CpuModified, register, render targets dirty, image tables invalidated, Nx2FastDraw::Break as
  DeleteImage does, its async decodes cancelled). Only while the clock's current frame began at an
  exact boundary (v2, below). Never for a shape whose GPU-written contents were sampled or
  rendered to without a clear in a later frame, or read by the CPU (sticky per shape, "Render
  target relocation guard" logs each first trip). EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_RELOCATE_AREA_COUNT=1 also
  counts a relocation in area_images_created, so EDEN_NX2_AREA_EVICT bursts fire as without it.
- EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_COLD=1: cold sweep, K = EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_COLD_FRAMES (unset/0: 4, upstream's
  default). Relocation-shaped BadOverlap chain members untouched for K host frames (LRU tick), no
  download queued, not bound, are deleted without a download in TickFrame, at most 16 a frame;
  GPU-written ones only when nothing says they are read again (EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_COLD_GUARDED=1 also
  sweeps shapes guarded as sampled/loaded, off as upstream).
- EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_CLOCK=1: with COLD alone, the guard's "this frame" follows the game's frame
  fence instead of TickFrame (with RELOCATE it is on anyway): nx2:syncpt TIPC 201 arms + nx2:vi2
  229 flips (nx2_gsv.h), bumped on the back in stream order in FenceManager::SignalSyncPoint.
  EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_CLOCK_SYNCPT=<id> overrides the source. Falls back to frame_tick when it stalls.
- EDEN_UP_FRITCHII_TOMBSTONE=1 (with RELOCATE): upstream EXP43=4 verify. Old ranges kept 2 render
  frames; reads landing on them are counted ("Relocation tombstones ... N hits", must stay 0),
  first 8 logged. A hit also guards its shape (second review below), so keep it on with RELOCATE.
Logs every 600 frames: "Render target relocation", "Cold sweep", "Render frame clock",
"Relocation tombstones".

v2 (review of fritchii-relocate.diff, each claim checked against T82 before fixing):
1. EDEN_NX2_READBACK_WAIT moved the frame boundary into the next frame. Confirmed: the hook fed
   guest value + 1, and held increments are not in the guest value, so with the frame's last two
   signals held both reported the same value, the boundary was missed and the next frame's first
   signal bumped it (host test readback_v1values on the v1 header: count 1 at the frame end, bumped
   inside the next frame). Fix: the hook feeds the stream position, guest value + the increments
   still held for that syncpoint (FenceManager::HeldIncrementsOf, on the back, where holds and
   releases happen: a release pops and makes the increment in one step, so the sum does not move);
   off the back with increments held the position is unknown: the clock is marked lost and
   relocation stops for the session (logged once). A flip that learns the syncpoint also starts
   from the back's latest stream position (NoteStream, seq_cst on both sides), not the lagging
   guest value, so the first boundary after a learn is not taken for "ahead" and bumped one
   signal late. Safety net: a bump at a signal past every value bumped on ("past their value") is
   inexact (below).
2. RELOCATE alone ran on the host-tick clock. Confirmed (CLOCK was a separate switch, off).
   Fix: RELOCATE turns the clock on, and a relocation needs RelocationBoundary: the clock live (not
   the frame_tick fallback), bumped since it went live (that step sits at a host tick), the latest
   bump exact, the clock not lost. g_nx2_render_frame now packs (bumps << 1) | inexact. Inexact:
   a signal past its value (it is inside the next frame), the frame an early bump may have split
   (marked when found), and a flip's own late bump. Upstream calls the late flip harmless; that
   holds only if the back ran none of the next frame's work before the flip arrived, which nothing
   here proves (other queues, a render thread that runs ahead), so it is a guest-thread-timed
   boundary like the host tick. One counter read per decision, so a guest-thread bump cannot land
   between the reliability check and the same-frame rule. Refusals are counted in the relocation
   line ("waited for an exact frame boundary"), inexact bumps in the clock line. If the logs show
   most frames start at late flips, a narrower rule (late flip with the back idle and no signal
   past the value) is the follow-up, not a relaxation of this one.
3. The bound-as-another-target check ignored rt_control.Map(). Not a live bug on this tree: our
   Vulkan path binds rt[index] for index < count (FindColorBuffer) and clears attachment
   clear_surface.RT; with count=2 rt[3] is not an attachment (a count change rebinds every slot), so
   the reviewer's case cannot occur. Hardened anyway, relocation-only: RelocationCandidate checks
   the slots index < count and Map(index) for both "bound as another target" and "render area
   smaller than the image" (overwritten_by_clear itself also drives the upload skip without
   relocation, so it stays as T82 has it).

Second review (2026-11-09, applied in place; the first v2 is fritchii-relocate-v2.diff.pre-review):
4. A cross-frame read that comes after the relocating clear in the same frame lands on a new image
   at the old address, never on the moved image, so the shape guard never trips and the same
   wrong relocation repeats every frame (only the tombstones see it). With TOMBSTONE=1 a hit now
   guards its shape (NoteTombstoneHit, one "relocation guard ... tombstone hit" line per shape and
   reason): at most the first wrong frame, the hit count still fails the arm.
5. A join copy into a new image sets GpuModified without MarkModification (last_write_frame stays
   ~0), so such an image passed the same-frame rule whatever frame its contents are from. It is
   now refused as written in this frame (counted with those). Rare for single-level targets.
   Rechecked (fritchii2-safety.review.log, VM scratch /tmp/rv-sr-q7m3): applies on T82 alone and
   stacked with fblimits + copybarrier in either order (+ churn/fbindex/unbacked on top), reverse
   dry-run, 6 anchor skips (the same), the main TUs rc=0, injected errors in both new blocks
   reported, clock_test passes its 6 scenarios.
Not changed, left for the logs: an armed bump on a mid-frame value of the learned slot ("early")
is exact until the next flip finds it (host test fritchii-relocate-v2_early_test.cpp: the clock
counts a frame at the mid-frame value with the flag clear, the flip then faults and marks the
frame inexact), so up to one real frame per session may be split before the armed source turns
off; after that nearly every bump is a late flip under the frame wait and relocation mostly
waits. "early" must stay 0.

Deviations from upstream: no EXP42 VkImage pool (not ported), no EXP43=1/2 modes (=3 only), no
rt_stats / slow-clear counters (see fritchii-churn.diff), no poll-word clock source (we have
svc_nx2_oot_poll, not svc_nx2_oot_present), no bindings_epoch (our fast-draw memo is invalidated by
the image-table Invalidate + Nx2FastDraw::Break), DeleteImage left as is (DropOverlapLinks is a
copy of its loop; DeleteImage only gains a cold_watch_set erase, a no-op on an empty set), our
SlotVector has no contains() so DeleteImage takes ids off the watch set, the PrepareImage hook
covers our texture memo (EDEN_UP_FAST_DRAW memo hits prepare through PrepareImage). The relocated
image's PrepareImage path is ours (IsFullClear decides the upload, as for a new image). With
EDEN_NX2_CLEAR_UPLOAD_GUARDS=1 (0235) a clear under a render condition is never a relocation
(clear_under_condition_keeps_upload). The v2 exactness rule and the stream-position hook are ours.

Checks (VM scratch /tmp/fr2-sr8k, fritchii2-safety_checks.sh, log fritchii2-safety.checks.log):
git apply --check on $R/Eglantine (T82); prepare-vulkan-port.py 6 anchor skips before and after,
the same ones; only the generated texture_cache.h copy differs, and it carries the DownloadMemory
guard, the RelocationBoundary call and the mapped-slot check; -fsyntax-only with the build's flags,
patched and baseline, of vk_texture_cache_base.cpp (Vulkan explicit instantiation),
vk_texture_cache_cost, vulkan_rasterizer, gl_texture_cache_base, texture_cache.cpp, image_base.cpp,
self_controller.cpp (nx2_gsv.h), vk_fence_manager_cost, gl_fence_manager, vk_buffer_cache_base,
nx2_fast_draw, gpu_split, nvhost_gpu, svc_nx2_oot_poll, maxwell_dma and the derived gpu,
gpu_thread, maxwell_3d, engines_puller, dma_pusher: all rc=0; static_asserts injected in the 7
touched headers all reported; v1's 15 injected calls and v2's 12 all reported (INJREL_join_inst
separately: the combined run stops reporting inside JoinImages after the first error there).
Host tests (clang++-18, the patched headers): claim_v1 on T82 + fritchii-relocate.diff reproduces
defect 1 (count 1 at the frame end, bumped by the next frame's first signal) and the learn race;
clock_test on v2 passes base, readback_v1values (the same bump, now flagged inexact),
readback_v2values (bump exactly at the frame end), late (a late flip holds relocation until the
next exact bump), learn_seed and lost; it does not compile against T82 or v1 (new API); a wrong
expectation fails it (rc 1). Stacked with fritchii2-fblimits, fritchii2-copybarrier and
fritchii-churn/-fbindex/-unbacked: applies, 6 anchor skips, the main TUs rc=0.

Expected effect on the PS5: fewer images/views/framebuffers made and collected per walk (t81-c
run546: 5,859 created / 4,850 deleted per 600 frames), fewer collector passes and downloads, lower
texture-cache memory (cold sweep: estimate 100-400 MiB); fps +0.3-1 docked at best (RADV image
creation is cheap; frames are fence-bound, and the guest CPU is now the limit). v2 relocates less
than v1 would: every frame that starts at a late flip (the back passed the fence value before the
present arrived, likely while the guest CPU is the bottleneck) waits for the next exact bump; the
two log lines show how often. Risk: one wrong frame if a relocated target's old contents are read
after the move (tombstones count it); AREA_EVICT burst timing changes (rerun the New Game route);
cold sweep drops GPU-written chain images without a copy. Not tested on the console.

